# Building a gnuplot-view for jmacs

:::HTML
<p class="subtitle">An interactive gnuplot notebook, embedded in the editor: one long-lived child process per view, a framed SVG submission protocol, and a custom element that turns each command into a themed, inline cell.</p>
<div class="meta"><span>L4 renderer view kind</span><span>main-process host + IPC bridge</span><span>pure protocol &amp; history modules</span></div>
</header>
:::

<!--TOC-->

## Motivation: an interactive gnuplot notebook inside jmacs

gnuplot is one of those tools you reach for in bursts. You want to see
`sin(x)`, then `sin(x), cos(x)`, then the same thing with a title and a tighter
`yrange` — each command a small refinement of the last. The natural shape of
that work is a /conversation/: a running session you talk to, where earlier
commands stay on screen and the next one builds on them. A one-shot
`gnuplot script.gp` invocation throws that context away every time. An
interactive terminal session keeps the process alive, but on most platforms
gnuplot's interactive plot lands in a separate OS window that floats outside
the editor, has its own dark/light assumptions, and disappears the moment you
plot again.

What we build in this walkthrough is the middle path: a *gnuplot view* that
lives /inside/ jmacs as a notebook REPL. One long-lived `gnuplot` child process
per view; an input line at the bottom; and a scrolling transcript above it where
every command you submit becomes a *cell* holding its result — an inline SVG
plot, text output from `print`/`show`, or a red error. The plot renders /in the
document/, themed to match the editor, and it stays put. Your previous five
plots scroll up; you can read your whole exploratory session at a glance.

This is what a real, themed plot looks like rendered inline — a bare
`plot sin(x), cos(x)` picking up the two leading palette colours:

:::HTML
<figure class="plot" data-plot="sincos"></figure>
<figcaption>A genuine gnuplot 6 plot at <code>set samples 2000</code>, dark background baked into the SVG, lines drawn in the project's <code>set linetype</code> palette (blue <code>#5aa9e6</code>, orange <code>#f78c6b</code>).</figcaption>
:::

The Lisp command that opens one is the entire user-facing surface:

```lisp
(defcommand gnuplot ()
  "Open a fresh gnuplot buffer.

   The buffer that appears is shown through the L4 gnuplot view (not the
   text editor view) — a notebook REPL. Type a gnuplot command at the
   input line and press Enter to run it; the plot (or text / error
   output) appears as a cell above. Use Up and Down to recall previous
   commands; press C-c to interrupt a long-running plot.

   Each call to `gnuplot` creates a new buffer — switch back to an open
   one by name with `C-x b`. If gnuplot is not installed, the view shows
   install instructions (`brew install gnuplot`)."
  (open-gnuplot-buffer!))
```

You invoke it with `M-x gnuplot` or the chord `C-c g`, bound by /symbol/ in the
`c-c-keymap` so the command resolves at dispatch time and load order never
matters:

```lisp
(define c-c-keymap
  {"tab"       'toggle-fold-at-point
   ;; ...
   ;; C-c g opens a gnuplot buffer (see gnuplot.lisp). Bound by symbol;
   ;; the command resolves at dispatch time, so load order doesn't matter.
   "g"         'gnuplot})
```

### Why a notebook, and why these specific design choices

The view deliberately mirrors jmacs's existing *shell view* — same
factory-plus-custom-element shape, same hide-not-kill lifecycle — but the
/interaction model/ is different, and that difference drives every decision in
the rest of this walkthrough. A shell streams raw pty bytes into an xterm grid;
there's no notion of "one command, one result." gnuplot in pipe mode /does/ have
that notion: you feed it a command, it draws a plot and maybe prints some text.
So instead of a terminal grid, the view is built from three parts:

```js
// instead of an xterm grid the view holds:
//   - a header (chart icon + buffer name),
//   - a scrollable transcript of per-command cells, and
//   - a bottom input line with its own command history (Up/Down).
```

Three consequences fall out of choosing a notebook over a terminal, and each is
a section of this walkthrough:

- *The host must frame each submission, not just forward bytes.* A terminal can
  pipe stdout through verbatim; a notebook needs to know when one command's
  output ends and the next begins. The host wraps every user command in a
  preamble/epilogue that routes the plot to a temp SVG file and emits two
  sentinel tokens, and a submission only /completes/ when both sentinels arrive.
- *The view needs its own command history.* The shell view keeps no history —
  the child shell's readline owns Up/Down. A bare `<input>` has no readline, so
  we implement a small history ring ourselves (and unit-test it without a DOM).
- *The plot must be themed by us, not by gnuplot's defaults.* gnuplot's stock
  SVG is white-on-white. To sit in a dark editor it has to be re-themed at render
  time — a dark background baked into the SVG, axis and grid colours, and a
  redefined line-type palette so a bare `plot sin(x), cos(x)` picks up two
  distinct, legible colours.

