A *cell* is created the moment you press Enter — echoed with its command and
marked /pending/ — and keyed by the submission id the host will later echo back.
When the result arrives, it's matched to that cell and filled in. This
echo-then-fill pattern is what makes the session feel responsive even though the
plot render is asynchronous:

```js
function runCommand(cmd) {
  if (!buffer || buffer.ended) return;

  const cell = doc.createElement('div');
  cell.className = 'gnuplot-cell gnuplot-cell-pending';
  // ...echo the command into the cell...
  transcriptEl.append(cell);
  scrollToEnd();

  // We track our own parallel counter so we can key the cell before the
  // result returns.
  buffer.submitCounter = (buffer.submitCounter ?? 0) + 1;
  const id = buffer.submitCounter;
  cellsById.set(id, cell);

  if (writeFn) {
    writeFn(buffer.sessionId, cmd).catch(() => {
      markCellError(cell, '[gnuplot write failed]');
    });
  }
}
```

A cell can end up as any one of four shapes — a plot, text, an error, or a dim
`(no output)` acknowledgement for a bare `set` — and the same result handler
decides which, based purely on what the host sent back in the
`{ svg, text, error }` payload. The plot SVG is inlined (not wrapped in an
`<img>` data-URL) so it /scales with the cell/; it's trusted-local markup with
`<script>` already stripped main-side:

```js
// The SVG is trusted-local (generated by the host's own gnuplot,
// scripts already stripped main-side); inline it so it scales with the cell.
if (typeof payload.svg === 'string' && payload.svg !== '') {
  const plot = doc.createElement('div');
  plot.className = 'gnuplot-cell-plot';
  plot.innerHTML = payload.svg;
  cell.append(plot);
}
```

### When gnuplot isn't installed

A view that hard-crashes when its backing tool is missing is a bad citizen in an
editor people open every day. gnuplot is frequently /not/ installed, and
`spawn('gnuplot', …)` doesn't even throw synchronously for a missing binary — it
emits an async `error` event with `code === 'ENOENT'` a tick later. The host
turns that into a distinct exit signal, and the view replaces its transcript with
a friendly install card and disables the input rather than showing a stack trace:

```js
if (payload.notInstalled) {
  transcriptEl.replaceChildren();
  cellsById.clear();
  const card = doc.createElement('div');
  card.className = 'gnuplot-not-installed';
  // ...chart icon + message...
  msg.textContent = payload.message ?? DEFAULT_NOT_INSTALLED;
  card.append(icon, msg);
  transcriptEl.append(card);
  input.disabled = true;
  input.placeholder = 'gnuplot unavailable';
  return;
}
```

> *Note.* The plot-rendering paths are unit-tested as pure logic (26 cases
> covering quoting, submission framing, the SVG-has-plot heuristic, the stream
> demux, and the history ring), and the protocol has been smoke-tested against a
> real gnuplot 6 — which is also what produced the genuine plots embedded
> throughout this page.

## Architecture: what a "view kind" actually is in jmacs

Before any gnuplot code, you have to understand the shape of the hole you are
filling. In jmacs, the editor does not have one canvas that everything draws
into. It has a family of /view kinds/ — text, image, pdf, browser, jukebox,
directory-tree, shell — and a new kind is not a special case bolted onto the
editor core. It is an assembly of four small, independently-replaceable parts
that already have well-defined seams. The discipline of the build was to add
/nothing new/ to the framework: every part of gnuplot slots into a socket that
the shell view defined first.

A view kind, concretely, is these four things:

1. *A renderer custom element* (L4) — a `<gnuplot-view>` DOM element that owns
   the on-screen surface and the user interaction. It knows nothing about
   subprocesses or IPC channel names; it is handed a bag of callbacks.
2. *A host configure-factory* — a plain function in `app.js`
   (`configureGnuplotView()`) that fills that bag of callbacks with the real IPC
   bindings, so the element can be tested with fakes and run with the real bridge.
3. *A main-process host* — the privileged side (`gnuplot.js`) that owns the
   actual `gnuplot` child process, the filesystem, and the dangerous powers the
   renderer is not allowed to touch.
4. *A Lisp surface* — the `(gnuplot)` command, a mode label, and a keybinding,
   which is how the kind becomes reachable to the user without hard-coding it
   into the host.

### The big picture

