### The renderer custom element — and why it owns no powers

The view is an HTML custom element, `<gnuplot-view>`, that extends a shared
`ViewElement` base. The element is deliberately ignorant: it never imports
`electron`, never names an IPC channel, never spawns anything. It is handed a
plain options object of callbacks at `configure()` time and it calls those. That
is the whole contract.

```js
export class GnuplotView extends ViewElement {
  configure(options) {
    if (this._inner !== null) {
      throw new Error('GnuplotView.configure: cannot reconfigure after mount');
    }
    this._options = options ?? null;
  }

  get kind() {
    return 'gnuplot';
  }

  connectedCallback() {
    if (this._inner !== null) return;
    this._inner = createGnuplotView(this, this._options ?? {});
    if (this._pendingBuffer !== null) this._inner.setBuffer(this._pendingBuffer);
  }
}
```

Why split `configure()` (set the callbacks) from `connectedCallback()` (build
the DOM)? Because a jmacs view is created once and /mounted many times/. The
element is constructed and configured eagerly at startup; the actual inner DOM
and history are not built until the element is first connected to the document.
`<shell-view>` has the identical configure-then-build split, for the same reason.

> *The load-bearing rule.* The renderer is sandboxed and untrusted. It cannot
> read files, cannot spawn processes, cannot run the SVG terminal. Everything
> dangerous lives behind the context bridge in the host. The view's only
> privilege is "ask the host to do something, and render what comes back." This
> is the Electron security model, and every view kind obeys it.

### The configure-factory — the IPC seam made explicit

The element wants callbacks; the host exposes IPC. The /configure-factory/ is
the one place those two are married. It is a plain function in `app.js` that
returns the options bag, wiring each renderer callback to the corresponding
`window.host.*` bridge method, with a graceful fallback when the bridge is absent:

```js
function configureGnuplotView() {
  return {
    spawn: (sessionId, opts) =>
      window.host && typeof window.host.gnuplotSpawn === 'function'
        ? window.host.gnuplotSpawn(sessionId, opts)
        : Promise.resolve({ ok: false, error: 'gnuplot IPC unavailable' }),
    write: (sessionId, data) =>
      window.host && typeof window.host.gnuplotWrite === 'function'
        ? window.host.gnuplotWrite(sessionId, data)
        : Promise.resolve({ ok: false }),
    signal: (sessionId) => /* … gnuplotSignal … */,
    kill:   (sessionId) => /* … gnuplotKill … */,
    onResult: (callback) => /* … onGnuplotResult … */,
    onExit:   (callback) => /* … onGnuplotExit … */,
    ...(keymapReady ? { onKey: dispatchKey } : {}),
  };
}
```

Put this next to `configureShellView()` and the shapes are almost identical —
both return `spawn / write / kill / onExit` plus a stream callback. Two
differences carry the whole design distinction between a terminal and a notebook:

- The shell returns `onData` — a /raw byte stream/; the gnuplot view returns
  `onResult` — a /structured, one-per-submission/ payload
  `{ id, svg, text, error }`. That single rename is the difference between
  "stream a pty into an xterm grid" and "submit a command, get back one cell."
- The shell carries `resize` (cols/rows for the pty); gnuplot has no pty, so
  there is no resize. Instead it carries `signal` (C-c → SIGINT to interrupt a
  running plot) and `onKey`, a chord-forwarding callback so the host keymap
  (C-x b to switch buffers) still fires while a plain `<input>` has focus.

### The IPC split and the context bridge

The renderer and the main process never share memory; they speak only over
Electron IPC, and the renderer can only reach IPC through the /context bridge/
set up in `preload.mjs`. The bridge exposes a small, named, audited surface — not
`ipcRenderer` itself. The gnuplot surface is six methods, split into two
directions:

```js
// renderer → host (invoke/return):
gnuplotSpawn:  (sessionId, options = {}) =>
  ipcRenderer.invoke('gnuplot:spawn', { sessionId, cwd: options.cwd }),
gnuplotWrite:  (sessionId, data) =>
  ipcRenderer.invoke('gnuplot:write', { sessionId, data }),
gnuplotSignal: (sessionId) =>
  ipcRenderer.invoke('gnuplot:signal', { sessionId }),
gnuplotKill:   (sessionId) =>
  ipcRenderer.invoke('gnuplot:kill', { sessionId }),

// host → renderer (subscribe, returns an unsubscribe fn):
onGnuplotResult: (callback) => {
  const handler = (_event, payload) => callback(payload);
  ipcRenderer.on('gnuplot:result', handler);
  return () => ipcRenderer.removeListener('gnuplot:result', handler);
},
onGnuplotExit:   (callback) => { /* same shape on 'gnuplot:exit' */ },
```

The asymmetry is deliberate and is the core of the IPC model. /Renderer → host
calls are request/response/ (`invoke` ⇒ `ipcMain.handle`): "spawn this," "submit
this command," "kill this" — each a one-shot with an acknowledgement. /Host →
renderer calls are an event stream/ (`webContents.send` ⇒ `ipcRenderer.on`):
results and exits arrive whenever the host has them. The `on*` subscriptions
return an /unsubscribe/ function — the view calls it on `destroy()`, which is how
the IPC listeners do not leak across the view's many mount/unmount cycles.

Each `write` is fire-and-forget at the IPC layer: it returns `{ ok }`
immediately, and the actual plot comes back asynchronously on `gnuplot:result`,
matched to its cell by the submission `id` the host echoes. This is why the view
keeps a `cellsById` Map. The shell uses the identical invoke/send split
(`shell:spawn/write/kill` inbound, `shell:data/exit` outbound); gnuplot did not
invent a new IPC discipline, only new channel names and a richer payload.

### Lifecycle: spawn, mount, kill — and "hide, don't kill"

A gnuplot view has three distinct lifecycles that are easy to conflate and must
be kept separate. Getting this wrong is the bug family that `docs/VIEWS.md` exists
to prevent.

*Spawn is lazy and tied to the first mount, not to creation.* The Lisp command
only creates a /buffer record/ with a fresh session id and `spawned: false`; no
process exists yet. The child is spawned the first time the view actually shows
the buffer:

```js
function setBuffer(next) {
  buffer = next;
  // …
  if (!buffer.spawned && !buffer.ended && spawnFn) {
    buffer.spawned = true;            // latch so we spawn exactly once
    spawnFn(buffer.sessionId, { cwd: buffer.cwd })
      .then((result) => { /* on !ok, mark ended + disable input */ })
      .catch(() => { /* host's onExit surfaces not-installed/error */ });
  }
}
```

The `spawned` flag lives on the buffer record, not the element, precisely because
the element is re-mounted as the user moves the tab around. If the latch lived on
the element it would re-fire and spawn a second process.

*Mount-moves hide, they do not kill.* When a tab is dragged out of view, jmacs
moves the element to an off-screen "warehouse," firing `disconnectedCallback`.
For a gnuplot view that callback is /intentionally empty/:

```js
/** Hide-not-kill — the gnuplot process stays alive; only the DOM-tree
 *  position changed. A later `connectedCallback` finds `_inner` set
 *  and is a no-op. */
disconnectedCallback() {
  /* intentionally empty */
}
```

This is the single most important invariant the shell view established and the
gnuplot view inherits: /a view's process and transcript outlive its on-screen
position/. If you reaped the process on `disconnectedCallback` you would get a
one-line "fix" that silently kills the user's session every time they look at
another tab.

*Only an explicit kill reaps the child.* The process dies when the /buffer/ is
killed (C-x k), which routes through one central place, `disposeKindView` in
`app.js`:

```js
function disposeKindView(view, context) {
  if (!view || typeof view.kind !== 'string') return;
  if (view.kind === 'shell')   { /* … window.host.shellKill(view.sessionId) … */ }
  if (view.kind === 'gnuplot') {
    if (typeof view.sessionId !== 'string') return;
    try {
      if (window.host && typeof window.host.gnuplotKill === 'function') {
        window.host.gnuplotKill(view.sessionId);
      }
    } catch { /* Process is already gone — nothing to do. */ }
    return;
  }
  // …
}
```

Keeping "free renderer memory" and "reap the OS process" as two different actions
is what lets hide-not-kill work at all: unmounting must be cheap and reversible,
while killing is final and explicit.

### The Lisp surface: how the kind becomes reachable

A view kind that the host can build is useless until a user can summon it, and in
jmacs that last mile is Lisp, not host code. A `gnuplot-mode` with the label
`"Gnuplot"` exists only to give the modeline something to show — its own comment
notes the buffer "is shown through the L4 gnuplot view … not the editor view, so
no text-buffer keymap applies." That a whole view kind is reachable, and
rebindable, entirely from the stdlib Lisp — without recompiling the host — is the
editor's extension story working as designed.

## Driving gnuplot: the long-lived pipe and the SVG protocol

By this point we have a `gnuplot`-kind buffer and a custom element to display it.
What we do /not/ yet have is the hard part: a reliable conversation with an
external program that was never designed to be embedded. gnuplot in pipe mode is
a line-oriented REPL. It reads commands from stdin, writes `print` output to
stdout and diagnostics to stderr, and — crucially — gives you /no framing/. There
is no "command N finished" marker, no exit code per command, no length prefix on
its output. It just talks until it has nothing more to say, and "nothing more to
say" looks exactly like "still thinking."

The job of this layer is to impose a request/response discipline on a stream that
has none, and to do it in a way that is testable without gnuplot even being
installed. That last constraint shaped the whole design, so it is worth stating
the split up front:

- *`apps/desktop/src/gnuplot-protocol.js`* — pure, DOM-free, Electron-free logic:
  how to /frame/ a submission, how to decide an SVG is real, and the stream
  demultiplexer that recognises when a submission is done. This is what the unit
  tests exercise.
- *`apps/desktop/src/gnuplot.js`* — the host glue: one long-lived child per
  session, temp-file capture, IPC, and the not-installed degradation path. It
  /uses/ the protocol module but contains none of its logic.

### One process per session, strictly serialized

Each gnuplot buffer owns exactly one gnuplot child, spawned lazily on first mount
and kept alive for the life of the buffer. We do /not/ spawn a fresh gnuplot per
command. That matters: gnuplot is stateful — `set`s, variables, and ranges
accumulate across commands — and a notebook REPL is only useful if
`set xrange [0:10]` still applies to the next `plot`.

```js
const child = spawn('gnuplot', [], {
  cwd,
  env,
  stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'pipe'],
  detached: false,
});
```

Two details in the spawn are load-bearing. First, `stdio` is three plain pipes —
no pseudo-terminal. gnuplot's pipe mode wants stdin/stdout/stderr, not a tty, so
unlike the shell view there is no PTY, no resize channel, and no native addon to
build. Second, the environment:

```js
// GNUTERM=unknown stops gnuplot trying to auto-select an interactive
// terminal (qt/x11/wxt) at startup. We set the real `svg` terminal per
// submission anyway.
const env = { ...process.env, GNUTERM: 'unknown' };
```

Submissions are strictly serialized. A user command is not written to stdin
directly; it is pushed onto a per-session queue and a `pump` only starts the next
job when the demultiplexer is idle:

```js
function pump(sessionId) {
  const entry = sessions.get(sessionId);
  if (!entry || entry.dead) return;
  if (entry.demux.busy || entry.queue.length === 0) return;
  // ... shift the job, build sentinels, set demux.begin({ onComplete }),
  //     write the framed program to gnuplot's stdin ...
}
```

Serialization is what makes the framing tractable. Because gnuplot processes its
stdin in order and we never have two submissions in flight, every byte that
arrives on stdout/stderr unambiguously belongs to the one active submission.

### The submission frame: preamble, user text, sentinel epilogue

Here is the heart of the protocol. Each user command is wrapped by
`buildSubmission` into a small gnuplot program. The user's text goes in /verbatim
and unmodified/ in the middle; everything around it is framing we control.

```js
export function buildSubmission(params) {
  const {
    userText, tmpFile, sentinelOut, sentinelErr,
    width = 720, height = 480, theme = DEFAULT_THEME,
  } = params;
  const t = themeFor(theme);
  const term =
    `set terminal svg size ${width},${height} dynamic enhanced ` +
    `background rgb '${t.background}' font 'sans,11'`;
  return [
    term,
    `set output ${quoteGnuplot(tmpFile)}`,
    t.block,                          // dark theme palette / axis colours
    userText,                         // verbatim, unmodified
    'unset output',                   // flush + close the SVG (mandatory)
    "set print '-'",
    `print ${quoteGnuplot(sentinelOut)}`,        // "submission complete" -> stdout
    "set print '/dev/stderr'",
    `print ${quoteGnuplot(sentinelErr)}`,        // "stderr flushed"       -> stderr
    "set print '-'",
    '',                               // trailing newline
  ].join('\n');
}
```

Reading the sequence top to bottom, each line earns its place. `set terminal svg`
re-declares the SVG generator on every submission — we don't trust the terminal
to persist across commands. `set output <tmpFile>` redirects the /plot bytes/ to
a temp file rather than to stdout — the single most important design choice in
the protocol, because routing the binary-ish plot to a file leaves stdout/stderr
carrying /only/ textual output and our sentinels. `unset output` is mandatory and
a classic gnuplot footgun: gnuplot buffers the SVG, and the file is not complete
(no closing `</svg>`) until the output is closed.

### Sentinel framing: completion gated on BOTH streams

Since gnuplot won't tell us when a command is done, we make it tell us — by
appending two `print` statements whose output we recognise. The tokens are
deliberately long and unguessable so they can't collide with real user output:

```js
export function sentinelsFor(id, nonce) {
  return {
    stdout: `__JMACS_GP_DONE__${id}__${nonce}__`,
    stderr: `__JMACS_GP_ERR__${id}__${nonce}__`,
  };
}
```

A private prefix, the submission id, and a fresh 6-byte random nonce per
submission. The subtle part is /why there are two sentinels, one per stream/.
gnuplot writes errors to stderr, which is a /separate/ pipe from stdout, and the
two are not synchronised — when the stdout sentinel arrives, there may still be
error bytes in flight on stderr. So the epilogue prints a sentinel down /each/
stream, and a submission is considered complete only when *both* sentinels have
been observed.

> *One trap, verified against gnuplot 6.* `set print '-2'` does /not/ mean
> "fd 2" — gnuplot opens a /file literally named "-2"/, so the stderr sentinel
> vanishes into that file and the submission hangs forever. The code uses
> `set print '/dev/stderr'` instead.

### The demultiplexer: a pure reducer over byte chunks

Pipes deliver bytes in arbitrary chunks. A sentinel can be split across two
`data` events; a single chunk can contain several lines. The `SubmissionDemux`
absorbs all of that. It is a small line-buffered state machine with no DOM and no
subprocess handle — which is exactly why it can be unit-tested against synthetic
chunk sequences.

```js
pushStdout(chunk) {
  this._outBuf += chunk;
  let nl;
  while ((nl = this._outBuf.indexOf('\n')) !== -1) {
    const line = this._outBuf.slice(0, nl);
    this._outBuf = this._outBuf.slice(nl + 1);
    const a = this._active;
    if (a && line === a.sentinelOut) {
      a.gotOut = true;
      this._maybeFinish();
    } else if (a) {
      a.stdoutLines.push(line);          // real user output
    }
  }
}
```

It only acts on /complete/ lines (terminated by `\n`); a trailing partial line
stays in `_outBuf` until the rest arrives. That is what makes a split sentinel
safe. Completion is gated on both flags:

```js
_maybeFinish() {
  const a = this._active;
  if (!a || !a.gotOut || !a.gotErr) return;   // need BOTH sentinels
  this._active = null;
  a.onComplete({
    stdout: a.stdoutLines.join('\n'),
    stderr: a.stderrLines.join('\n'),
  });
}
```

### Capturing the SVG temp file vs. text and errors

When both sentinels land, the demux fires its `onComplete`, which the host wired
to `finishSubmission`. Now the host turns three independent channels — the temp
file, stdout text, stderr text — into one result payload. Note that the SVG never
travelled over a pipe; it was written to disk, and we read it back here:

```js
function finishSubmission(sessionId, id, tmpFile, stdout, stderr) {
  const entry = sessions.get(sessionId);

  let svg = null;
  try {
    const st = statSync(tmpFile);
    const text = readFileSync(tmpFile, 'utf8');
    if (svgHasPlot(st, text)) {
      // Strip any <script> gnuplot's svg terminal might emit, as
      // defence-in-depth before the renderer inlines the markup.
      svg = stripScripts(text);
    }
  } catch {
    // No file -> no plot. Leave svg null.
  }
  // Read-and-delete: the SVG bytes now live in the result payload.
  unlink(tmpFile, () => {});

  if (entry) entry.active = null;
  if (entry && !entry.sender.isDestroyed()) {
    entry.sender.send('gnuplot:result', {
      sessionId, id, svg, text: stdout, error: stderr,
    });
  }
  pump(sessionId);   // start the next queued submission
}
```

A command need not produce a plot. `print "hi"` writes to stdout and never opens
an output file; `set xrange [0:10]` produces nothing at all. So `svg` is `null`
unless a real plot file exists. "Real plot" is a heuristic, not a guess —
`svgHasPlot` rejects a file that's too small, that lacks a closing tag
(truncated), or that has no drawn element:

```js
export function svgHasPlot(stat, text) {
  if (!stat || typeof stat.size !== 'number') return false;
  if (stat.size <= EMPTY_SVG_THRESHOLD) return false;            // 512 bytes
  if (typeof text !== 'string') return false;
  if (!text.includes('</svg>')) return false;                    // not truncated
  return /<(path|rect|polyline|circle|image|use|text)\b/.test(text);  // drawn
}
```

Measured against gnuplot 6, `plot sin(x)/x` at `set samples 2000` emits an SVG of
tens of KB built from dozens of `<path>` elements — far above the 512-byte
threshold — while a `set`-only submission stays a few hundred bytes. Here is one
such real result:

:::HTML
<figure class="plot" data-plot="sinc"></figure>
<figcaption><code>plot sin(x)/x</code> over <code>[-20:20]</code>. The smooth ripples are real samples, not a hand-drawn polyline — the SVG is dozens of <code>&lt;path&gt;</code> segments produced by gnuplot itself.</figcaption>
:::

### Dark-theme SVG settings

A plot has to read on a dark editor page, and it has to do so without depending
on the host page's CSS — because the SVG is also a standalone artifact a user
might save. So the theme is baked into the image itself, in two parts. First, the
page background is set on the terminal line so the SVG carries its own dark
canvas:

```js
/** The page background baked into the SVG itself, so the plot is a
 *  self-contained dark image that doesn't depend on the host page's CSS. */
export const SVG_BACKGROUND = THEMES.dark.background;   // '#1e2228'
```

Second, a block of `set` directives recolours every chrome element — border,
tics, title, axis labels, key, grid — and then redefines the first six line types:

```js
dark: {
  label: 'Dark',
  background: '#1e2228',
  block: [
    "set border lc rgb '#7f8c98'",
    "set xtics textcolor rgb '#aeb6c0'",
    "set ytics textcolor rgb '#aeb6c0'",
    "set ztics textcolor rgb '#aeb6c0'",
    "set title  textcolor rgb '#e6e9ed'",
    "set xlabel textcolor rgb '#cfd6de'",
    "set ylabel textcolor rgb '#cfd6de'",
    "set key textcolor rgb '#cfd6de'",
    "set grid lc rgb '#3a434d' lw 1",
    "set linetype 1 lc rgb '#5aa9e6' lw 2 pt 7",
    "set linetype 2 lc rgb '#f78c6b' lw 2 pt 7",
    "set linetype 3 lc rgb '#7ed491' lw 2 pt 7",
    "set linetype 4 lc rgb '#c792ea' lw 2 pt 7",
    "set linetype 5 lc rgb '#ffcb6b' lw 2 pt 7",
    "set linetype 6 lc rgb '#ff6b8a' lw 2 pt 7",
  ].join('\n'),
},
```

The choice of `set linetype` (rather than the deprecated `set style increment
user`) is deliberate: redefining the line types means a /bare/ `plot sin(x),
cos(x)` — with no explicit `ls` on either curve — automatically picks up the
first two palette colours. Verified on gnuplot 6: the first line emits as
`stroke='rgb( 90, 169, 230)'` — i.e. `#5aa9e6`, linetype 1 from the theme block.
The whole six-colour palette, exercised by `plot sin(x)+k` for k = 0..5:

:::HTML
<figure class="plot" data-plot="palette"></figure>
<figcaption>Six stacked sine curves, each picking up the next <code>set linetype</code> colour in order: <code>#5aa9e6</code>, <code>#f78c6b</code>, <code>#7ed491</code>, <code>#c792ea</code>, <code>#ffcb6b</code>, <code>#ff6b8a</code> — exactly the editor's accent palette, baked into the plot.</figcaption>
:::

Because the colours are inside the SVG, the renderer's `applyTheme` is a
deliberate no-op — there is nothing on the page to recompute when the editor
theme changes, and the CSS only has to make the embedded SVG scale:

```css
.gnuplot-cell-plot svg {
  display: block;
  max-width: 100%;
  height: auto;
}
```

### Interrupting a running plot

A heavy plot (a fine `samples` count over a slow function) can take real time.
Pressing `C-c` in the input line sends `SIGINT` to the child:

```js
function signalSession(sessionId) {
  const entry = sessions.get(sessionId);
  if (!entry || entry.dead) return false;
  try {
    return entry.child.kill('SIGINT');   // already-queued epilogue then runs
  } catch {
    return false;
  }
}
```

Because the epilogue (`unset output` plus the two sentinel `print`s) is
/already/ queued in gnuplot's stdin, an interrupted plot still drives both
sentinels and the cell completes cleanly — usually as an error or empty cell
rather than a plot — instead of leaving a pending cell stuck forever. The
interrupt cooperates with the framing rather than bypassing it.

## The session and history data model

Everything the gnuplot view shows on screen is reconstructed from three small,
deliberately boring data structures: the *cell* (one submitted command and its
eventual result), the *command-history ring* (the Up/Down recall buffer behind
the input line), and the per-session *submission queue* that lives in the host.
None of these is a framework abstraction. They are plain objects, a `Map`, and an
array, and that plainness is the point.

### A cell is the unit of the transcript

The shell view streams raw pty bytes into an xterm grid; there is no notion of a
"result" there, just an ever-scrolling character buffer. gnuplot is different:
each command you submit produces /one/ result — a plot, or some text, or an error.
So the gnuplot view's transcript is not a character stream but a column of
discrete *cells*, each created the instant you press Enter and filled in later
when the host answers.

A cell is a tiny state machine. It is created `pending`, then `onResult` resolves
it into exactly one terminal shape based on which of `svg` / `text` / `error` the
payload carries — and a cell may carry several of these at once (a plot /and/ a
warning). The CSS classes name the states directly:

| State (CSS class) | Set when | What the cell shows |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `gnuplot-cell-pending` | Cell created, awaiting the result | Just the echoed command, dimmed (`opacity: 0.7`) |
| `gnuplot-cell-plot` | `payload.svg` is a non-empty string | The dark-themed SVG, inlined and scaled to the cell |
| `gnuplot-cell-stdout` | `payload.text` is non-blank | `print` / `show` output, foreground colour |
| `gnuplot-cell-error` / `-stderr` | `payload.error` is non-blank | Red text, red cell border (a typo, a warning) |
| `gnuplot-cell-empty` | None of the above produced output | A dim italic `(no output)` placeholder |

The resolution logic in `onResult` reads almost like the table — it inspects the
payload field by field, appends a region for each, and falls back to an explicit
empty acknowledgement so a bare `set` command never leaves a blank cell:

```js
function onResult(payload) {
  if (!payload || typeof payload !== 'object') return;
  if (!buffer || payload.sessionId !== buffer.sessionId) return;
  const cell = cellsById.get(payload.id);
  if (!cell) return;
  cellsById.delete(payload.id);
  cell.classList.remove('gnuplot-cell-pending');

  let rendered = false;
  // The plot, if any. The SVG is trusted-local (generated by the
  // host's own gnuplot, scripts already stripped main-side); inline it
  // so it scales with the cell.
  if (typeof payload.svg === 'string' && payload.svg !== '') {
    const plot = doc.createElement('div');
    plot.className = 'gnuplot-cell-plot';
    plot.innerHTML = payload.svg;
    cell.append(plot);
    rendered = true;
  }
  // Text output (print, show, ...).
  if (typeof payload.text === 'string' && payload.text.trim() !== '') {
    appendRegion(cell, 'gnuplot-cell-stdout', payload.text);
    rendered = true;
  }
  // Errors / warnings.
  if (typeof payload.error === 'string' && payload.error.trim() !== '') {
    cell.classList.add('gnuplot-cell-error');
    appendRegion(cell, 'gnuplot-cell-stderr', payload.error);
    rendered = true;
  }
  // A bare `set` with no output: show a dim acknowledgement.
  if (!rendered) {
    appendRegion(cell, 'gnuplot-cell-empty', '(no output)');
  }
  scrollToEnd();
}
```

Notice that the three result fields are checked independently, not as a `switch`.
A submission like `plot sin(x)` that also emits a deprecation warning will produce
a cell that is /both/ a plot and an error. The `cellsById.delete(payload.id)` at
the top is what makes a cell single-shot: once resolved, its id is gone, and a
duplicate result for the same id is silently dropped.

> *Why key by id at all, if submissions are serialized?* The host runs one
> gnuplot child per session and processes submissions strictly in order, so
> results /do/ arrive in order. Keying by id is belt-and-suspenders: it makes the
> renderer correct regardless of arrival order, and a stray or duplicate result
> simply finds no cell (`if (!cell) return;`) instead of corrupting the
> transcript. Robustness for the cost of one `Map`.

### The command-history ring

The input line is a plain `<input>`. It has no readline, no ZLE, nothing that
gives a shell its Up/Down recall. The shell view doesn't need to care — xterm.js
hands raw bytes to the child shell, whose own readline owns the arrow keys. The
gnuplot view has no such luxury, so it implements history itself, as a small
DOM-free module that can be unit-tested without a browser. The model is the
standard REPL triple — a list of entries, a cursor, and a stashed draft:

```js
export function createHistory() {
  /** @type {string[]} */
  const entries = [];
  let index = 0;
  let draft = '';

  return {
    push(command) {
      if (typeof command === 'string' && command.trim() !== '') {
        entries.push(command);
      }
      index = entries.length;
      draft = '';
    },

    up(current) {
      if (entries.length === 0) return current;
      if (index === entries.length) draft = current;
      if (index > 0) index -= 1;
      return entries[index];
    },

    down(current) {
      if (index >= entries.length) return draft;
      index += 1;
      if (index === entries.length) return draft;
      return entries[index];
    },

    reset() { index = entries.length; draft = ''; },
    entries() { return entries.slice(); },
    index() { return index; },
  };
}
```

The cursor invariant is the whole trick: `index === entries.length` means "you
are on the live, not-yet-submitted line." Stepping `up` from the live line first
stashes whatever you had half-typed into `draft`, then walks the cursor toward
older entries, clamping at the oldest. Stepping `down` walks back toward newer
entries, and when it passes the newest it restores the stashed draft rather than
blanking the input.

> *The draft is what separates this from a naive history.* Without it, arrowing
> up mid-type and then back down would silently destroy the line you were writing.
> The `up` guard `if (index === entries.length) draft = current` captures it on
> the /first/ step only. The full ring has nine unit tests covering push/up/down,
> draft excursions, blank-skip, cursor reset, and that `entries()` hands back a
> copy that can't mutate internal state.

### How a submission flows through

With the cell and the ring in place, a single Enter keypress threads a whole
round trip. On the host side, a `write` does not run gnuplot directly — it
appends to a per-session queue and pumps it. This is where serialization is
enforced:

```js
function writeToSession(sessionId, data) {
  const entry = sessions.get(sessionId);
  if (!entry || entry.dead) return false;
  entry.counter += 1;
  entry.queue.push({ id: entry.counter, userText: String(data) });
  pump(sessionId);
  return true;
}
```

The host's `counter` is the source of truth for the id; the renderer's
`buffer.submitCounter` shadows it so the two agree without a round trip. That
message — `{ sessionId, id, svg, text, error }` — is the seam between the two
halves of the model. The host built it by demultiplexing a byte stream; the
renderer consumes it in `onResult`, matches `id` back to the pending cell, and
resolves the cell into its final state. Small enough to reason about in one line,
rich enough to express every cell state.

> *Sessions, not a global.* Each `gnuplot` command opens a fresh buffer with its
> own `sessionId` (minted by `nextGnuplotSessionId()` as
> `gnuplot-<n>-<timestamp>`), and the host keys its `sessions` `Map` by that id.
> The view's `onResult`/`onExit` handlers both guard on
> `payload.sessionId !== buffer.sessionId`, so two open gnuplot buffers never
> cross-talk even though they share the same IPC channel.

## The view element: a notebook over a long-lived gnuplot child

Everything up to here has been plumbing: the host spawns one `gnuplot` per
session, frames each submission, and sends back a single `gnuplot:result`
carrying `{ id, svg, text, error }`. The /view element/ is where that becomes
something a person looks at. Its whole job is to turn a stream of those results
into a scrolling *notebook transcript*: you type a command at the bottom, press
Enter, and a *cell* appears above holding the rendered plot, any text output, or a
red error.

The file is `packages/renderer/src/gnuplot-view.js`. It follows the same
two-layer shape every L4 singleton view in jmacs uses — a plain
`createGnuplotView(container, options)` factory that builds the DOM and wires the
behaviour, wrapped by a `GnuplotView extends ViewElement` custom element that
gives it the hide-not-kill lifecycle.

### The DOM skeleton

The factory builds the three regions once, at construction time. Note the
deliberate ordering in the flex column — header, transcript, input row — and that
the transcript is the only one allowed to grow and scroll:

```js
const root = doc.createElement('div');
root.className = 'gnuplot-view';
root.tabIndex = -1;
container.append(root);

// Header: a chart icon + the buffer name.
const header = doc.createElement('div');
header.className = 'gnuplot-header';
const headerIcon = doc.createElement('i');
headerIcon.className = 'fa-solid fa-chart-line';
const headerLabel = doc.createElement('span');
headerLabel.className = 'gnuplot-header-label';
headerLabel.textContent = 'gnuplot';
header.append(headerIcon, headerLabel);
root.append(header);

// Transcript: a scrolling column of per-command cells.
const transcriptEl = doc.createElement('div');
transcriptEl.className = 'gnuplot-transcript';
root.append(transcriptEl);

// Input line: a prompt + a plain text input.
const inputRow = doc.createElement('div');
inputRow.className = 'gnuplot-input-row';
const prompt = doc.createElement('span');
prompt.className = 'gnuplot-prompt';
prompt.textContent = 'gnuplot>';
const input = doc.createElement('input');
input.type = 'text';
input.className = 'gnuplot-input';
input.spellcheck = false;
inputRow.append(prompt, input);
root.append(inputRow);
```

The input is a bare `<input type="text">`: no contenteditable, no fake cursor, no
terminal emulation. That bareness is the whole reason the view has to provide its
own history. Note `doc` and `win` are taken from `container.ownerDocument` rather
than the global `document` — a small discipline that keeps the factory testable
and correct if the view is ever mounted into another window.

### Filling the cell: text and errors

Text and error regions go through a tiny shared helper, which is the only place a
`<pre>` is created. Using `textContent` (never `innerHTML`) for text and errors
means gnuplot's output can never inject markup — only the trusted SVG path uses
`innerHTML`:

```js
/** Append a text region to a cell, returning the created element. */
function appendRegion(cell, className, text) {
  const pre = doc.createElement('pre');
  pre.className = className;
  pre.textContent = text;
  cell.append(pre);
  return pre;
}
```

A notebook REPL wants the newest cell visible. The view does not track scroll
position — it simply calls `scrollToEnd` after every mutation that adds height:

```js
/** Scroll the transcript to the bottom. */
function scrollToEnd() {
  transcriptEl.scrollTop = transcriptEl.scrollHeight;
}
```

This works because the CSS makes the transcript the /only/ scrollable region. The
flex column gives the transcript `flex: 1 1 auto` and the crucial
`min-height: 0`, so it shrinks to fit and scrolls internally while the header and
input row stay pinned.

### The input line, history, and chord forwarding

The view wires the history ring to the input's `keydown` handler, which is also
where the chord-forwarding and the C-c interrupt live. Enter and the arrows are
handled locally; everything else that looks like a chord is forwarded to the host
keymap:

```js
input.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
  if (event.key === 'Enter') {
    event.preventDefault();
    const cmd = input.value;
    if (cmd.trim() !== '') {
      history.push(cmd);
      runCommand(cmd);
    }
    input.value = '';
    history.reset();
    return;
  }
  if (event.key === 'ArrowUp') {
    event.preventDefault();
    input.value = history.up(input.value);
    win.requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      input.setSelectionRange(input.value.length, input.value.length);
    });
    return;
  }
  if (event.key === 'ArrowDown') { /* …mirror of ArrowUp… */ }

  // C-c interrupts a running plot (the shell-view analog of SIGINT).
  if (event.key === 'c' && event.ctrlKey && !event.metaKey && !event.altKey) {
    if (buffer && signalFn && !buffer.ended) {
      event.preventDefault();
      signalFn(buffer.sessionId);
      return;
    }
  }
  // Forward chords (C-x b, M-x, …) to the host keymap. Plain typing
  // and the modifier keys themselves fall through to the input.
  if (onKey) {
    const key = keyEventToString(event);
    if (key && onKey(key)) event.preventDefault();
  }
});
```

The last branch is the bridge back to the editor: because the input is a real
focusable `<input>`, the host keymap would otherwise go dark while you're typing
in it. Forwarding non-printing chords through `onKey` keeps `C-x b`, `M-x`, and
friends alive — exactly the trick the browser view uses for its URL bar. Enter,
the arrows, and `C-c` are handled locally and deliberately /not/ forwarded.

### Theming is a deliberate no-op

jmacs's other singleton views recompute colours when the theme changes — the
shell view re-reads CSS variables and pushes them into xterm. The gnuplot view is
the interesting exception: its `applyTheme` is a deliberate no-op.

```js
/** Re-theme. The plot SVGs bake their own dark background at render
 *  time, so there's nothing to recompute here; kept for parity with
 *  the singleton view interface. */
function applyTheme() {
  /* no-op — SVGs are self-themed at render time */
}
```

The dark theme of a plot is not applied by the renderer at all — it is baked into
the SVG by the /host/ at render time. The method still exists, empty, so the view
satisfies the same `{ setBuffer, focus, applyTheme, destroy }` interface as every
other singleton view and can be registered uniformly. One consequence worth
flagging: a live theme switch will /not/ re-colour plots already in the
transcript; only newly rendered cells pick up the new palette.

### The custom-element wrapper and lifecycle

The factory knows nothing about being mounted, hidden, or moved between panes.
That is the custom element's job. `GnuplotView extends ViewElement` is a thin
adapter: `configure(options)` stashes the IPC wiring before mount,
`connectedCallback` lazily builds the inner factory on first attach, and — the
important bit — `disconnectedCallback` is intentionally empty:

```js
export class GnuplotView extends ViewElement {
  get kind() { return 'gnuplot'; }

  connectedCallback() {
    if (this._inner !== null) return;
    this._inner = createGnuplotView(this, this._options ?? {});
    if (this._pendingBuffer !== null) this._inner.setBuffer(this._pendingBuffer);
  }

  /** Hide-not-kill — the gnuplot process stays alive; only the DOM-tree
   *  position changed. A later `connectedCallback` finds `_inner` set
   *  and is a no-op. */
  disconnectedCallback() {
    /* intentionally empty */
  }

  /** Explicit teardown: drop the IPC subscriptions. The host's
   *  kill-view path reaps the gnuplot process separately. */
  destroy() {
    if (this._inner !== null) { this._inner.destroy(); this._inner = null; }
    this._pendingBuffer = null;
  }
}
```

> *Hide-not-kill is the whole point of the empty `disconnectedCallback`.* Moving
> a pane to the warehouse, or splitting and rearranging panes, fires
> `disconnectedCallback` on the element. If that tore down the view, the user
> would come back to an empty transcript and a fresh gnuplot process — their plots
> gone. So disconnect does nothing, and a later `connectedCallback` finds `_inner`
> already set and short-circuits. The gnuplot /process/ is only reaped on explicit
> `destroy()` plus the host-side kill path. This split is the rule documented in
> `docs/VIEWS.md`: the DOM position is owned by the pane tree, the process
> lifetime is owned by the host, and the two are deliberately decoupled.

## Command history and recall

Every REPL needs a way to bring back what you just typed. You run `plot sin(x)`,
eyeball the curve, then want `plot sin(x), cos(x)` — and you do not want to retype
the whole line. The fix is the same one every shell and language REPL has shipped
for decades: /Up/ walks backward through the commands you submitted, /Down/ walks
forward again, and whatever the arrows land on is dropped into the input where you
can edit it and re-run it.

The interesting question for the gnuplot view is not /whether/ to have history —
it is /who owns it/.

### Why this view has to implement history itself

jmacs already has a terminal view (shell v4), and it has full command history —
Up/Down, reverse-search, the lot. But none of that history code lives in jmacs.
The shell view is an `xterm.js` grid that forwards raw keystrokes straight to a
child shell; the shell's own `readline`/`ZLE` line editor owns the history ring.
jmacs is just a dumb pipe in both directions. It never sees a "command" — only
bytes.

The gnuplot view is the opposite shape. Its input line is a plain `<input>`
element, and gnuplot's own command line does not run inside it. There is no
`readline` in the loop to remember anything. So if this view is going to have
Up/Down recall, the view has to keep the ring itself. The file header states
exactly this:

```js
/**
 * @file A small, DOM-free command-history ring for the gnuplot notebook
 * view's input line. The shell v4 view keeps no history of its own —
 * xterm.js hands raw bytes to the child shell, whose readline/ZLE owns
 * Up/Down. The gnuplot view has a bare `<input>` with no readline, so it
 * must implement history itself. Extracting it as pure logic keeps it
 * unit-testable without a DOM (per CLAUDE.md's "unit-test pure logic").
 */
```

> *The jmacs pattern.* Where a child process owns the line editor, forward bytes
> and keep no state. Where jmacs owns the input element, jmacs owns the history.
> The browser view's URL bar follows the same rule. This file is the gnuplot view
> paying the price of owning its input — but it pays it in twelve lines of pure
> logic, not a re-implemented line editor.

### The cursor model

The live line is a real position one past the newest entry; that is where `draft`
lives.

:::HTML
<figure>
<svg viewBox="0 0 600 200" role="img" aria-label="History cursor model: entries array with the live-line slot one past the end">
  <text x="10" y="24" class="d-label">entries[]</text>
  <rect x="10"  y="36" width="120" height="44" rx="4" fill="#232a31" stroke="#3a434d"/>
  <rect x="130" y="36" width="120" height="44" rx="4" fill="#232a31" stroke="#3a434d"/>
  <rect x="250" y="36" width="120" height="44" rx="4" fill="#232a31" stroke="#3a434d"/>
  <rect x="370" y="36" width="160" height="44" rx="4" fill="#232a31" stroke="#5aa9e6"/>
  <text x="70"  y="63" class="d-text" text-anchor="middle">"plot sin(x)"</text>
  <text x="190" y="63" class="d-text" text-anchor="middle">"plot cos(x)"</text>
  <text x="310" y="63" class="d-text" text-anchor="middle">"replot"</text>
  <text x="450" y="58" class="d-text" text-anchor="middle">live line</text>
  <text x="450" y="73" class="d-dim" text-anchor="middle">(the draft)</text>
  <text x="70"  y="100" class="d-dim" text-anchor="middle">index 0</text>
  <text x="190" y="100" class="d-dim" text-anchor="middle">1</text>
  <text x="310" y="100" class="d-dim" text-anchor="middle">2</text>
  <text x="450" y="100" class="d-dim" text-anchor="middle">3 = entries.length</text>
  <text x="300" y="160" class="d-text" text-anchor="middle">Up moves the cursor left (older) · Down moves it right (newer)</text>
  <text x="300" y="184" class="d-dim" text-anchor="middle">Down off the right-hand end returns to the live line and restores the stashed draft</text>
</svg>
<figcaption>The history ring's three pieces of state: an <code>entries</code> array, a cursor <code>index</code> that may legally point one slot past the end, and the <code>draft</code> that lives at that live-line slot.</figcaption>
</figure>
:::

### up and down: the recall logic

The two arrow methods take the input's /current/ text as an argument. That is
deliberate — it is how the draft gets captured at the exact moment you leave the
live line, without the history needing any reference to the DOM.

```js
up(current) {
  if (entries.length === 0) return current;
  if (index === entries.length) draft = current;
  if (index > 0) index -= 1;
  return entries[index];
},

down(current) {
  if (index >= entries.length) return draft;
  index += 1;
  if (index === entries.length) return draft;
  return entries[index];
},
```

Reading `up` line by line: `if (entries.length === 0) return current` — Up on an
empty history is a no-op. `if (index === entries.length) draft = current` — this
fires /only/ on the first Up from the live line; step Up again from inside history
and this is false, so an earlier-stashed draft is never clobbered.
`if (index > 0) index -= 1` — walk one step older, but clamp at the oldest entry.
`down` is the mirror image; its subtlety is the live line — stepping forward past
the newest entry lands you back on the live line and hands back the `draft` you
stashed.

### Why a separate, DOM-free module

The ring could have lived as three closure variables inside `createGnuplotView`.
Pulling it out into `gnuplot-history.js` buys one concrete thing: it is testable
with no browser, no `jsdom`, no custom element. The whole behaviour — including
the fiddly bits people get wrong — is nailed down by nine `node:test` cases that
import the function directly:

```js
test('down walks back toward the live line and restores the stashed draft', () => {
  const h = createHistory();
  h.push('first');
  h.push('second');
  // Start typing a new line, then arrow up — the draft is stashed.
  assert.equal(h.up('half-typed'), 'second');
  assert.equal(h.up('half-typed'), 'first');
  // Walk back down: second, then the live line (the draft).
  assert.equal(h.down('first'), 'second');
  assert.equal(h.down('second'), 'half-typed');
});

test('entries returns a copy that cannot mutate internal state', () => {
  const h = createHistory();
  h.push('a');
  const snapshot = h.entries();
  snapshot.push('hacked');
  assert.deepEqual(h.entries(), ['a']);
});
```

> *Design takeaway for jmacs.* When a view owns its own input element, the
> line-editing logic is yours to write — but keep it DOM-free and pure. The
> element stays a thin adapter (read `input.value`, write `input.value`, park the
> caret); the behaviour lives where it can be tested in milliseconds without a
> render. The same split is why the protocol framing and the `svgHasPlot` check
> are their own pure modules too.

## Wiring the view kind into jmacs

By this point the two halves exist in isolation: a main-process host
(`apps/desktop/src/gnuplot.js`) that spawns and drives a `gnuplot` child, and a
renderer custom element (`packages/renderer/src/gnuplot-view.js`) that draws the
notebook. Neither knows about the other, and neither is reachable from inside the
editor. /Wiring/ is the seam that joins them: it makes `gnuplot` a first-class
*view kind* alongside `text`, `image`, `shell`, and the rest.

There is no plugin manifest and no registry to append to. In jmacs a view kind is
wired by touching a fixed set of seams, each of which answers one question the
shell asks at a different moment.

