### The Lisp surface: command, keymap, mode

The user-facing entry point is a Lisp command. It is deliberately thin — it
documents the feature and delegates to a single primitive.

```lisp
(defcommand gnuplot ()
  "Open a fresh gnuplot buffer.
   ...
   Each call to `gnuplot` creates a new buffer — switch back to an open
   one by name with `C-x b`. If gnuplot is not installed, the view shows
   install instructions (`brew install gnuplot`)."
  (open-gnuplot-buffer!))
```

The keybinding lives under the `C-c` prefix map. Note the binding is to the
/symbol/ `'gnuplot`, not to the procedure value — so load order between the keymap
and the command definition doesn't matter; the symbol resolves at dispatch time.
A `gnuplot-mode` gives the buffer a modeline label (the view has no text buffer,
so this is cosmetic), and `gnuplot.lisp` is added to `STDLIB_FILES` so it loads at
startup:

```lisp
;; A `gnuplot`-kind buffer is shown through the L4 gnuplot view (a
;; notebook REPL), not the editor view, so no text-buffer keymap applies.
(define-mode gnuplot-mode
  :name "Gnuplot")
```

> A regression test in `packages/stdlib/test/stdlib.test.js` pins all three facts
> at once — the command loads, it is documented, and `C-c g` resolves to it:
> `(procedure? gnuplot)` is true, `(doc gnuplot)` is a string, and
> `(eq? (get c-c-keymap "g") (quote gnuplot))` holds.

### The primitive: open-gnuplot-buffer!

The Lisp command calls one host primitive, defined in `app.js`. Its job is to
mint a session id, build a `View` of kind `'gnuplot'`, push it onto the global
`views` list, and switch the focused pane to it. Crucially, it does /not/ spawn
the process — that happens on mount.

```js
'open-gnuplot-buffer!': () => {
  const sessionId = nextGnuplotSessionId();
  const sequence = views.filter((v) => v.kind === 'gnuplot').length + 1;
  const name = sequence === 1 ? '*gnuplot*' : `*gnuplot*<${sequence}>`;
  views.push(createView({
    kind: 'gnuplot',
    name,
    extras: {
      sessionId,
      ended: false,
      spawned: false,
    },
  }));
  switchToViewIndex(views.length - 1);
  return NIL;
},
```

The session id generator is a monotonic counter combined with a timestamp,
mirroring the shell view's scheme:

```js
let gnuplotSessionCounter = 0;
function nextGnuplotSessionId() {
  gnuplotSessionCounter += 1;
  return `gnuplot-${gnuplotSessionCounter}-${Date.now()}`;
}
```

### The singleton and SINGLETON_VIEWS

jmacs mounts each non-text view kind as a *single* custom-element instance — a
singleton — created once at startup and moved between panes by show/hide rather
than rebuilt:

```js
const gnuplotView = /** @type {*} */ (document.createElement('gnuplot-view'));
gnuplotView.configure(configureGnuplotView());
editorPaneElement().append(gnuplotView);
gnuplotView.style.display = 'none';
themeListeners.add(() => gnuplotView.applyTheme());
```

It is then registered in `SINGLETON_VIEWS`, the single source of truth the shell
iterates to toggle display and release resources. The `releasesBuffer: true` flag
marks gnuplot as media-bearing alongside `audio`, `video`, and `shell`:

```js
const SINGLETON_VIEWS = [
  { kind: 'customize',         el: customizeView,         releasesBuffer: false },
  { kind: 'image',             el: imageView,             releasesBuffer: false },
  // ...
  { kind: 'shell',             el: shellView,             releasesBuffer: true  },
  { kind: 'gnuplot',           el: gnuplotView,           releasesBuffer: true  },
];
```

A tabline that needs a /fresh/ per-tab instance asks `perKindConfigureFactory`
for the recipe — the same `configureGnuplotView` factory, reused so each per-tab
`<gnuplot-view>` gets its own freshly-bound callbacks and its own session:

```js
function perKindConfigureFactory(kind) {
  switch (kind) {
    // ...
    case 'shell':             return configureShellView;
    case 'gnuplot':           return configureGnuplotView;
    default:                  return null;
  }
}
```

### Kill on close

Killing a buffer routes through `disposeKindView`, the mirror of the mount path.
Most kinds have nothing to clean up, but a gnuplot view owns an OS process, so its
branch sends a `gnuplotKill` over the bridge to reap the child and drop its temp
directory:

```js
if (view.kind === 'gnuplot') {
  if (typeof view.sessionId !== 'string') return;
  try {
    if (window.host && typeof window.host.gnuplotKill === 'function') {
      window.host.gnuplotKill(view.sessionId);
    }
  } catch {
    // Process is already gone — nothing to do.
  }
  return;
}
```

On the host side `gnuplot:kill` lands in `terminateSession`, which `SIGTERM`s the
child, `rm -rf`s the per-session temp dir, and forgets the entry. The contract is
closed in both directions: every `spawn` on mount has a matching `kill` on close,
keyed by the session id that the primitive minted and that travelled untouched
through the View's `sessionId` the whole way.

### The seams, in one list

For a future view kind, this is the checklist the gnuplot wiring instantiates —
each item answers a question the shell asks at a specific moment:

- *preload bridge* — how does the renderer reach the host?
  (`gnuplotSpawn/Write/Signal/Kill`, `onGnuplotResult/Exit`)
- *configure factory* — how is a view instance handed its host callbacks?
  (`configureGnuplotView`)
- *singleton + `SINGLETON_VIEWS`* — what instance shows this kind, and does it
  release resources when hidden?
- *`perKindConfigureFactory`* — how does a tabline build a fresh per-tab instance?
- *open primitive* — how does Lisp create a view of this kind?
  (`open-gnuplot-buffer!`, plus `nextGnuplotSessionId`)
- *command + keymap + mode* — what does the user type?
  (`gnuplot`, `C-c g`, `gnuplot-mode`)
- *spawn on mount* — flag-gated in `setBuffer`, dispatched by `mountKindView`.
- *kill on close* — the `disposeKindView` branch.

Miss any one and the failure is specific and visible: skip the `SINGLETON_VIEWS`
row and the view never hides; skip the `disposeKindView` branch and processes
leak; skip the `perKindConfigureFactory` case and it works as a leaf but breaks
the moment it lands in a tabline tab.

## Testing, graceful degradation, and where to take it next

A subprocess-driven view is mostly /untestable in a unit test/ if you write it
naively: spawning a real `gnuplot`, waiting on async IPC, diffing rendered SVG
bytes — none of that runs under a plain `node --test`. So the design question that
governs this whole feature is: *how much of it can be made pure?* The more logic
that lives in DOM-free, Electron-free, subprocess-free functions, the more of it a
fast test suite pins down, and the smaller the residue that genuinely needs a
human at a running app.

### The pure/live split, by design

Everything that can be reasoned about without a live subprocess was pulled out of
the host module (`gnuplot.js`, which holds Electron and `child_process` and is
/not/ unit-tested) into a sibling that imports nothing but JavaScript:
`apps/desktop/src/gnuplot-protocol.js`. The same move happens on the renderer
side: the command-history ring is lifted into
`packages/renderer/src/gnuplot-history.js` as a `createHistory()` factory with no
DOM dependency at all.

