<figcaption>The boundary, drawn once: pure modules are <code>gnuplot-protocol.js</code> and <code>gnuplot-history.js</code>; the impure residue is <code>gnuplot.js</code> and <code>gnuplot-view.js</code>. The split is exactly the line between "26 fast assertions" and "a few things to check in the running app."</figcaption>
</figure>
:::

### What the pure assertions pin down

The protocol suite (17 cases) and the history suite (9 cases) cover the parts
that are easy to get subtly wrong. Quoting is one: the temp-file path is
interpolated into `set output '…'`, and gnuplot's single-quoted strings do /not/
interpret backslashes, so the safe escape is to double embedded single quotes and
otherwise pass bytes through verbatim — which means a Windows-style path with
backslashes must survive untouched:

```js
test('quoteGnuplot single-quotes and doubles embedded quotes', () => {
  assert.equal(quoteGnuplot('/tmp/a.svg'), "'/tmp/a.svg'");
  assert.equal(quoteGnuplot("o'brien"), "'o''brien'");
  assert.equal(quoteGnuplot(''), "''");
});

test('quoteGnuplot does not interpret backslashes (gnuplot single-quote semantics)', () => {
  // A Windows-ish path with backslashes survives verbatim.
  assert.equal(quoteGnuplot('C:\\tmp\\a.svg'), "'C:\\tmp\\a.svg'");
});
```

The both-sentinels gate is the heart of the framing protocol: a submission is
"done" only when /both/ the stdout and the stderr sentinel have arrived. The test
asserts the demux waits, and that a sentinel cut across a chunk boundary still
reassembles:

```js
demux.pushStdout('hello\nworld\n');
demux.pushStdout('OUT\n'); // stdout sentinel — but stderr not yet in
assert.equal(result, null, 'must wait for BOTH sentinels');
demux.pushStderr('ERR\n');
assert.deepEqual(result, { stdout: 'hello\nworld', stderr: '' });
assert.equal(demux.busy, false);
```

### The gnuplot-not-installed path

gnuplot is genuinely often missing, and the design choice is to make that a
/first-class outcome/, not an error in the transcript. The tricky part is that
`spawn('gnuplot', …)` does *not* throw synchronously for a missing binary — it
resolves, then emits an async `error` event a tick later with
`code === 'ENOENT'`. So the spawn IPC handler returns `{ ok: true }` immediately
and the not-installed signal arrives separately:

```js
child.on('error', (error) => {
  entry.dead = true;
  sessions.delete(sessionId);
  rm(tmpDir, { recursive: true, force: true }, () => {});
  if (sender.isDestroyed()) return;
  if (error.code === 'ENOENT') {
    sender.send('gnuplot:exit', {
      sessionId, code: null, signal: null,
      notInstalled: true,
      message: NOT_INSTALLED_MESSAGE,
    });
    return;
  }
  sender.send('gnuplot:exit', {
    sessionId, code: null, signal: null,
    error: error.message,
    message: `gnuplot failed to start: ${error.message}`,
  });
});
```

The renderer's exit handler keys on the `notInstalled` flag, /replaces/ the
transcript with a centred install card, and disables the input — there is no point
letting the user type at a process that will never answer. The host owns the
canonical message (with `brew install gnuplot` and the Debian/Ubuntu line), and
the renderer carries an identical `DEFAULT_NOT_INSTALLED` fallback.

> *Defence in depth on the SVG.* Adjacent to degradation is a second safety
> property: gnuplot's svg terminal can emit a `<script>` (its interactive mousing
> layer). The host strips it before the renderer inlines the markup —
> `svg.replace(/<script\b[\s\S]*?<\/script>/gi, '')` — so even though the SVG is
> trusted-local it never reaches the DOM with executable content. The same strip
> is applied to every plot embedded in this very page.

### A damped oscillation, end to end

A last real plot — a multi-curve `plot` with an explicit `key`, exactly the kind
of exploratory command the notebook is built for:

:::HTML
<figure class="plot" data-plot="damped"></figure>
<figcaption><code>plot exp(-x/6)*cos(x), exp(-x/6), -exp(-x/6)</code>. Three curves, two named in the key, picking up linetypes 1–3 of the dark palette — a genuine gnuplot 6 render at 2000 samples, themed and embedded statically.</figcaption>
:::

### Where to take it next

The architecture leaves several natural extensions cheap to add, because the hard
parts (one persistent process, serialized submissions, the framing protocol) are
already in place. In rough order of value:

- *Data files.* A gnuplot session's `cwd` already threads through:
  `spawnSession` honours `options.cwd` (defaulting to `homedir()`), and the view
  passes `buffer.cwd` on spawn. Plotting a local `data.dat` should work once `cwd`
  is wired to the active project directory rather than home — a one-line change at
  the call site, no protocol change.
- *Export.* The result payload already carries the raw SVG string back to the
  renderer (`payload.svg`). A "save plot as…" affordance is a renderer-only
  addition: write that string to disk, or re-run the last submission with
  `set terminal pngcairo` / `pdfcairo` for a non-dark export.
- *Multiple sessions.* This already works: every `(gnuplot)` call mints a fresh
  `sessionId` and a fresh `*gnuplot*` buffer, each owning its own child process.
  What is /not/ yet built is a session picker; today you navigate them by buffer
  name.
- *Replot on resize.* This is the one genuinely missing capability. The SVG is
  rendered once at a fixed `720×480` and then CSS-scaled to the cell width. A true
  replot-on-resize would mean re-issuing the /last/ submission for that cell at the
  new pixel width — but stateful sessions make "re-run the last line" subtly wrong,
  and a debounce would be needed to avoid a replot storm while dragging a divider.
  This is a design decision, not a typo-fix, and it should be flagged to the
  architect rather than guessed.

> *The through-line.* Every extension above is cheap precisely because of the
> pure/impure split this section opened with. New behaviour lands in
> `buildSubmission` (a pure function with tests) or in the renderer view (DOM,
> observable in the running app), and the messy middle — spawn, framing, temp
> files, reaping — does not have to change. That is what "tested where it can be,
> honest where it can't" buys you.
